      <div class="guide-section">
        <div>
          <h2>Override</h2>
          <p class="guide-lede">
            Override Mode is for runs where some or most of the SFINCS input files already exist.
            Instead of building everything from source data, you select native SFINCS files from a static folder and/or an event folder, let the launcher detect them, choose which ones to use, and then finish the remaining setup in a simplified manual-style workflow.
          </p>
        </div>
    
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>Static files vs. event files</h3>
          <p>
            Override Mode separates native SFINCS inputs into two practical groups: static files and event files. Static files describe the physical makeup of the model domain that is mostly constant in time. Event files describe the forcing or initial conditions for one specific storm, flood, tide period, or scenario.
          </p>
          <p>
            This split matters because you often want to reuse the same physical model setup for many different events. For example, the topography, bathymetry, mask, roughness, boundary locations, source locations, and observation locations may stay the same, while the rainfall, water-level boundary, discharge time series, wind, pressure, or restart state changes event to event.
          </p>
        
          <div class="guide-grid-2">
            <div class="mini-card">
              <h4>Static files</h4>
              <p>
                Static files describe things inherent to the model area or model design. These are the pieces that usually do not change just because the event changes.
              </p>
              <p>
                Examples include topography/bathymetry, active-cell masks, grid index files, subgrid tables, roughness, infiltration layers, boundary/source geometry, observation points, cross-sections, and structures.
              </p>
            </div>
        
            <div class="mini-card">
              <h4>Event files</h4>
              <p>
                Event files describe what happens during a specific run period. These are usually time-dependent files or initial-state files.
              </p>
              <p>
                Examples include water-level boundary time series, discharge time series, rainfall, wind, atmospheric pressure, wave-related forcing, and restart or initial water-level files.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        
          <div class="callout">
            <p>
              <strong>Note:</strong> this Override guide focuses on the special file-detection and override workflow. For a deeper review of the normal launcher tabs and settings, use the Manual section of this guide.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
    
        <figure class="image-placeholder">
          <strong>Image 1 — Override folder selection</strong>
          Add a screenshot showing the Static folder and Event folder inputs before detection. The example can show a Harvey 2017 setup where the static folder supplies the fixed model files and the event folder supplies the event-specific files.
        </figure>
    
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>How Override differs from Manual</h3>
          <p>
            Override Mode is basically Manual Mode with an extra native-file step at the beginning. In Manual Mode, you mostly enter settings directly. In Override Mode, you first choose static and/or event folders, detect native SFINCS files, and select which detected files should override the normal generated inputs.
          </p>
          <p>
            After the override files are selected, the page becomes closer to a stripped-down Manual Mode. You still choose the run name, output folder, timing, resources, advanced settings, review checks, and submit behavior. The difference is that some settings may already be controlled by selected native files.
          </p>
          <p>
            When a selected native file controls part of the setup, the matching manual control may become hidden, locked, or greyed out. This is intentional. It prevents the page from suggesting that a setting is editable when the chosen override file is actually controlling it.
          </p>
        
          <div class="callout">
            <p>
              <strong>Important:</strong> detecting a file is not always the same as using it. Detection finds possible files. The selected override list decides which files are actually copied into the run and pointed to by the generated <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>Normal Override workflow checklist</h3>
          <ul class="workflow-checklist">
            <li>
              <span>1</span>
              <div>Choose the static native SFINCS folder and/or event native SFINCS folder.</div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>2</span>
              <div>Click Detect Files and confirm the expected files appear.</div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>3</span>
              <div>Select the detected files that should actually be used as overrides.</div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>4</span>
              <div>Review hidden, locked, or greyed-out controls so you understand what the selected files now control.</div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>5</span>
              <div>Set the run name, output folder, timing, resources, and remaining editable settings.</div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>6</span>
              <div>Inspect <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span>-related settings and Advanced Config before running.</div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>7</span>
              <div>Read the review checks and fix missing-file, invalid-config, timing, or overwrite warnings before moving on.</div>
            </li>
            <li>

              <span>8</span>

              <div>Optionally use Save Config if you want to pause, make a reusable template, or later restore the setup with Load Config.</div>

            </li>

            <li>

              <span>9</span>

              <div>Run preflight. Preflight validates the setup and records the current page config/scaffold for the run workflow.</div>

            </li>

            <li>

              <span>10</span>

              <div>Build the Slurm scripts and confirm the scaffold was created correctly.</div>

            </li>

            <li>

              <span>11</span>

              <div>Submit the run only after the folders, overrides, timing, resources, and warnings look correct.</div>

            </li>

            <li>

              <span>12</span>

              <div>After the jobs finish, check logs and outputs such as <span class="inline-code">sfincs_map.nc</span>, <span class="inline-code">sfincs_his.nc</span>, and postprocessing products.</div>

            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
    
    
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>Native SFINCS files available for override</h3>
          <p>
            These tables are a launcher-oriented reference for native SFINCS files. They are not meant to teach every file from scratch. If you are using Override Mode, the assumption is that your files already came from a trusted SFINCS setup, preprocessing workflow, or previous run.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some files are normally static, some are normally event-specific, and a few can be either depending on the workflow. The launcher should eventually expose all of these as possible native override options, but a project setup may only use a small subset.
          </p>
        
          <h4>Static / physical setup files</h4>
          <div class="guide-table-wrap">
            <table class="guide-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>File / pointer</th>
                  <th>Usual role</th>
                  <th>Usually changes event to event?</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.dep</code> / <code>depfile</code></td>
                  <td>Topography/bathymetry or depth/elevation at grid cells.</td>
                  <td>No, unless rebuilding the physical domain.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.msk</code> / <code>mskfile</code></td>
                  <td>Active/inactive/boundary-cell mask.</td>
                  <td>No.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.ind</code> / <code>indexfile</code></td>
                  <td>Index file used with binary grid-style inputs.</td>
                  <td>No.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.sbg</code> or subgrid NetCDF / <code>sbgfile</code></td>
                  <td>Subgrid table information for subgrid SFINCS runs.</td>
                  <td>No.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.manning</code>, <code>sfincs.man</code> / <code>manningfile</code></td>
                  <td>Spatially varying Manning roughness.</td>
                  <td>No, unless roughness assumptions change.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.qinf</code> / <code>qinffile</code></td>
                  <td>Spatially varying constant infiltration rate.</td>
                  <td>Usually no.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.scs</code> / <code>scsfile</code></td>
                  <td>Curve Number infiltration layer.</td>
                  <td>Usually no.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.smax</code> / <code>smaxfile</code></td>
                  <td>Maximum soil moisture storage for Curve Number recovery method.</td>
                  <td>Usually no.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.seff</code> / <code>sefffile</code></td>
                  <td>Effective soil moisture storage at start.</td>
                  <td>Sometimes; may be event/initial-condition dependent.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.ks</code> / <code>ksfile</code></td>
                  <td>Saturated hydraulic conductivity for infiltration methods.</td>
                  <td>Usually no.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.sigma</code> / <code>sigmafile</code></td>
                  <td>Green-Ampt suction-head related infiltration input.</td>
                  <td>Usually no.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.psi</code> / <code>psifile</code></td>
                  <td>Green-Ampt soil moisture deficit input.</td>
                  <td>Usually no.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.f0</code> / <code>f0file</code></td>
                  <td>Horton maximum initial infiltration capacity.</td>
                  <td>Usually no.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.fc</code> / <code>fcfile</code></td>
                  <td>Horton minimum/asymptotic infiltration capacity.</td>
                  <td>Usually no.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.kd</code> / <code>kdfile</code></td>
                  <td>Horton decay constant.</td>
                  <td>Usually no.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.vol</code> / <code>volfile</code></td>
                  <td>Spatially varying storage volume, such as green infrastructure storage.</td>
                  <td>Usually no.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        
          <h4>Geometry, output-request, and structure files</h4>
          <div class="guide-table-wrap">
            <table class="guide-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>File / pointer</th>
                  <th>Usual role</th>
                  <th>Usually changes event to event?</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.bnd</code> / <code>bndfile</code></td>
                  <td>Water-level boundary point locations.</td>
                  <td>Usually no.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.src</code> / <code>srcfile</code></td>
                  <td>Source/discharge point locations.</td>
                  <td>Usually no.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.obs</code> / <code>obsfile</code></td>
                  <td>Observation point locations for history output.</td>
                  <td>Usually no, but can change for validation-output reruns.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.crs</code> / <code>crsfile</code></td>
                  <td>Cross-section lines for discharge output.</td>
                  <td>Usually no, but can change for validation-output reruns.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.thd</code> / <code>thdfile</code></td>
                  <td>Thin dams, treated like fully blocking barriers.</td>
                  <td>No.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.weir</code> / <code>weirfile</code></td>
                  <td>Weirs or levee-like barriers with a height and coefficient.</td>
                  <td>No.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.drn</code> / <code>drnfile</code></td>
                  <td>Drainage pumps, culverts, or check valves.</td>
                  <td>Usually no, unless testing structure scenarios.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        
          <h4>Event forcing and initial-state files</h4>
          <div class="guide-table-wrap">
            <table class="guide-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>File / pointer</th>
                  <th>Usual role</th>
                  <th>Usually changes event to event?</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.bzs</code> / <code>bzsfile</code></td>
                  <td>Water-level boundary time series.</td>
                  <td>Yes.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>sfincs.bzi</code> / <code>bzifile</code></td>
                  <td>Quickly varying water-level or wave-related boundary time series.</td>
                  <td>Yes.</td>
