        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>Step-by-step Override workflow</h3>
    
          <ol class="guide-steps">
            <li>
              <strong>Choose the static and/or event folders.</strong>
              Start by selecting the folder that contains the static native files, the folder that contains the event native files, or both. For example, a Harvey 2017 example might use a trusted static model folder plus a Harvey-specific event folder.
            </li>
    
            <li>
              <strong>Detect available native files.</strong>
              Use the detection button to scan the selected folders. This lets the page find files such as <span class="inline-code">sfincs.dep</span>, <span class="inline-code">sfincs.msk</span>, <span class="inline-code">sfincs.bzs</span>, <span class="inline-code">sfincs.dis</span>, and <span class="inline-code">precip_2d.nc</span>.
            </li>
    
            <li>
              <strong>Select the files you actually want to override with.</strong>
              Detection only finds candidates. The selected override list is what tells the launcher which native files should be copied into the run folder and referenced by the generated model input.
            </li>
    
            <li>
              <strong>Review fields that become hidden, locked, or greyed out.</strong>
              If a selected native file already supplies part of the model, the corresponding manual control may no longer matter. In that case, the launcher may hide it, lock it, or grey it out so the page does not imply that changing the field will affect the run.
            </li>
    
            <li>
              <strong>Set the run identity and output location.</strong>
              Choose a clear run name and a run output folder. The run folder is where the launcher writes the final config, scripts, logs, copied native inputs, SFINCS outputs, and postprocessing outputs.
            </li>
    
            <li>
              <strong>Check the timing and model settings.</strong>
              If a detected <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> is available, the launcher may use it to fill timing fields like reference time, start time, and stop time. Even when <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> itself is overridden, the model settings and advanced config area should stay visible because those settings are important to inspect before running.
            </li>
    
            <li>
              <strong>Set resources and submit behavior.</strong>
              Choose the Slurm resources for preprocessing, SFINCS, and postprocessing. The correct values depend on model size, event length, output frequency, and how heavy postprocessing will be.
            </li>
    
            <li>
              <strong>Use Advanced Config only for settings you understand.</strong>
              Advanced Config is for SFINCS keys or backend settings that are not exposed as normal page controls. This is useful for expert edits, but it expects valid syntax and assumes you know what the keys mean.
            </li>
    
            <li>
              <strong>Run the review checks.</strong>
              Before submitting, review the detected files, selected overrides, model timing, output folder, overwrite behavior, resources, and any warnings. Page-level checks are helpful, but the backend preflight is still the real check before a run should be submitted.
            </li>
    
            <li>

              <strong>Use Save/Load only when useful; then preflight, build, and submit.</strong>

              Save Config is mainly for pausing your work, making a reusable template, or coming back later with Load Config. You do not need to manually save before a normal run. Running backend preflight records the current page setup and creates the config/scaffold needed for the run workflow. Build Scripts prepares the Slurm scripts, and Submit launches the jobs. A scaffold is not completed model output; real outputs only exist after the jobs finish.

            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">

          <h3>Save Config and Load Config</h3>

          <p>

            Save Config and Load Config are convenience tools, not required steps for every run. Use Save Config when you want to pause and come back later, create a reusable template, duplicate a setup, or share the setup with someone else.

          </p>

          <p>

            Load Config restores a saved JSON setup back into the page. This is useful when you want to continue a previous setup without re-entering all folders, overrides, timing, resources, and advanced settings by hand.

          </p>

          <p>

            For a normal one-off run, you do not need to click Save Config before running. Running backend preflight sends the current page setup to the backend and records the config/scaffold needed for that run workflow.

          </p>

        </div>




    
        <figure class="image-placeholder">
          <strong>Image 3 — Hidden or greyed-out controls</strong>
          Add a screenshot showing a setting that becomes locked, hidden, or greyed out because a selected native override file already controls that part of the setup.
        </figure>
    
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>About <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> in Override Mode</h3>
          <p>
            <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> is different from the other native files because it controls the run settings and points to the other input files. In Override Mode, the safer pattern is usually to let the launcher generate a fresh <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> from the page settings, while using detected native files for the model inputs.
          </p>
          <p>
            You may still use a detected <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> as a reference for timing or settings. Directly overriding <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> should be treated as an intentional reproduction choice, not the default beginner path.
          </p>
    
          <div class="callout">
            <p>
              <strong>Why the settings stay visible:</strong> even when <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> is selected or parsed, the model settings and Advanced Config areas remain visible so you can inspect important values before running.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
    
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>Advanced Config</h3>
          <p>
            Advanced Config is a raw settings area for keys that are not represented by the normal page controls. Use it for deliberate SFINCS or backend settings that you understand, such as projection behavior, numerical controls, output options, or specialized forcing switches.
          </p>
          <p>
            The main rule is simple: if you are not sure what a key does, do not change it here. A typo, wrong value type, missing comma, or outdated setting can change the run or make it fail before SFINCS starts.
          </p>
        </div>
    
        <figure class="image-placeholder">
          <strong>Image 4 — Advanced Config and model settings</strong>
          Add a screenshot showing the model settings and Advanced Config area. The screenshot should make clear that this section is for checking or editing important SFINCS settings, not for casual changes.
        </figure>
    
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>Review checks</h3>
          <p>
            The review section is the “slow down and check yourself” part of the workflow. It should summarize the run name, output folder, selected static/event folders, detected files, selected overrides, timing, resources, overwrite behavior, and any warnings.
          </p>
          <p>
            These warnings are general checks. They do not mean every run is wrong, but they point to things that deserve attention before you submit. Missing file pointers, suspicious empty fields, invalid JSON, overwrite warnings, and mismatched static/event choices should not be ignored.
          </p>
        
          <div class="guide-table-wrap">
            <table class="guide-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Review item</th>
                  <th>What it is checking</th>
                  <th>Why it matters</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Run name and output folder</td>
                  <td>Where the run will be written.</td>
                  <td>Prevents accidentally overwriting or confusing runs.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Static/event folder choices</td>
                  <td>Which native files are being pulled from which folders.</td>
                  <td>Prevents mixing the wrong physical setup with the wrong event.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Selected override files</td>
                  <td>Which detected files are actually active overrides.</td>
                  <td>Detection alone does not mean a file is being used.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Timing fields</td>
                  <td>Reference time, start time, stop time, and output intervals.</td>
                  <td>Bad timing can make forcing files misalign or shorten the run accidentally.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Advanced Config</td>
                  <td>Raw advanced settings and JSON syntax.</td>
                  <td>A typo or invalid value can break the run or change model behavior.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>File-pointer checks</td>
                  <td>Whether files referenced by <code>sfincs.inp</code> will exist in the run folder.</td>
                  <td>SFINCS should not be submitted with missing declared inputs.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
        
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>Preflight, build scripts, and submit</h3>
          <p>

            The submit section turns the reviewed page settings into backend actions. Treat Preflight, Build Scripts, and Submit as separate steps, not one giant button.

          </p>

          <p>

            Run backend preflight first. Preflight validates the current setup and records the config/scaffold needed by the run workflow. Build Scripts then prepares the Slurm scripts. Submit launches the Slurm chain.

          </p>
        
          <div class="guide-table-wrap">
            <table class="guide-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Button / action</th>
                  <th>What it does</th>
                  <th>What to check</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>

                  <td>Preflight</td>

                  <td>Runs backend validation and records the current page setup for the run workflow.</td>

                  <td>Fix errors before moving on. Warnings should be read, not skipped blindly.</td>

                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Build scripts</td>
                  <td>Creates the run-folder scaffold and Slurm scripts.</td>
                  <td>A scaffold is normal. It is not completed model output.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Submit</td>
                  <td>Submits the Slurm workflow for preprocessing, SFINCS, and postprocessing.</td>
                  <td>Only submit after the config, review checks, and output folder look correct.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Check status/logs</td>
                  <td>Looks at job status, launcher logs, Slurm logs, and model outputs.</td>
                  <td>If a job fails, logs are usually the fastest way to identify the failed stage.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        
          <div class="callout">
            <p>
              <strong>Scaffold vs. real output:</strong> after Build Scripts, it is normal to see folders like scripts, logs, model, and postprocess. That does not mean the model has run yet. Real outputs appear only after the submitted jobs complete.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
    
